    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "`answer()` is now in `app/chat.py`, and all 6 tests pass: the 2 that were already there plus 4 new ones.\n\n**How it works:**\n- Each attempt runs `iter_answer` and joins the pieces only after the stream finishes. If an attempt drops partway, its partial text is thrown away, so the reply you get back always comes from one complete attempt.\n- The installed openai SDK turns every network failure into `APIConnectionError`, whether it happens when connecting or partway through the stream. The error message is always the generic \"Connection error.\", and the real network error is attached as its cause. So each WARNING logs that underlying error's type and message, for example: `Attempt 2/3 to reach the model failed: RemoteProtocolError: peer closed connection without sending complete message body`. That lets ops tell connection resets from truncated responses.\n- In this SDK a timeout error is a special kind of connection error, so the code checks for timeouts first. A timeout raises `UpstreamTimeout` straight away with no retry.\n- If all 3 attempts fail, it raises `UpstreamUnavailable`, with the last error attached as its cause.\n- `history` is turned into a list once at the start. If a caller passes a one-time iterator, each retry still sends the full conversation.\n- The client is used as given, and no dependencies were added.\n\n**Tests added (`tests/test_chat.py`):**\n- A normal stream is joined correctly.\n- A failure on connect, then a stream that drops partway, then a success returns only the successful text, logs 2 warnings containing the underlying error messages, and sends the full history every time.\n- 3 failures in a row raise `UpstreamUnavailable`.\n- A timeout partway through the stream raises `UpstreamTimeout` after a single call.\n\nThe tests use fake clients that raise errors the same way the SDK does. I read that behaviour from the installed SDK's code but didn't test it against a real connection."
  },
